//
// Normative rule cbo-inval_s_mode_enabled_op (src/priv/supervisor.adoc):
//   If CBO.INVAL is enabled in S-mode to perform a flush operation, then when the instruction is
//     enabled in U-mode it performs a flush operation, even if CBIE is set to 0b11. Otherwise, the
//     instruction behaves as follows, depending on the CBIE encoding: 01 the instruction is
//     executed and performs a flush operation; 11 the instruction is executed and performs an
//     invalidate operation.
//
// Context:
//   senvcfg (0x10A) exists from privileged spec 1.12 (S1P12P0_OR_LATER_SUPPORTED). menvcfg is
//     reached through T-SBI. The framework boots with menvcfg and senvcfg CBIE=11, CBCFE=1, CBZE=1;
//     tests restore that. CBO instructions are .word encodings with rs1=x6 (CBO.ZERO
//     0x0040200F|rs1<<15, CBO.CLEAN 0x0010200F|..., CBO.FLUSH 0x0020200F|..., CBO.INVAL
//     0x0000200F|...). Their operand is a 4 KiB-aligned, 4 KiB data block used for nothing else, so
//     it holds any cache block size up to 4 KiB. A flush keeps the newest data; an invalidate may
//     discard a dirty copy, so a later load reads either the newest data or the data in memory
//     before the store. The block starts as 0x1111111111111111 in memory; S-mode stores a new
//     value, U-mode runs CBO.INVAL, and S-mode loads it back. CBIE values are those read back
//     (WARL).
//
// Test plan:
//   For (menvcfg.CBIE, senvcfg.CBIE) written (01, 11), (01, 01), (11, 01), (11, 11), with a
//     different new value each time: after the U-mode CBO.INVAL, the word reads the new value when
//     either field reads 01 (a flush), and the new value or the value loaded before the store when
//     both read 11 (an invalidate). If either field reads back 00 (WARL), the U-mode CBO.INVAL must
//     raise illegal-instruction instead.
//
// Approximate dynamic instruction count: 3349
//

